Mild Rheumatoid Arthritis Seronegative
Complex Fatigue Entities
Inflammatory arthritis lacking RF/CCP

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-033
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe hand/wrist swelling, excruciating joint pain, and inability to perform basic self-care tasks.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, dramatic exacerbation of wrist and finger joint swelling, rendering the patient completely unable to grip objects, open medication, or prepare food. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out acute soft tissue infection (cellulitis/tenosynovitis) and provide potent anti-inflammatory rescue therapy to manage severe functional impairment prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My hands are swollen like sausages, the pain is relentless, and I am too exhausted to even open a bottle of water.
History of Present Illness: Patient with suspected seronegative arthritis reports a rapid 48-hour escalation of joint pain and swelling. The timeline demonstrates progressively worsening morning stiffness that now lasts over 3 hours, evolving into constant, deep aching in the wrists, MCP, and PIP joints bilaterally. Symptoms are highly responsive to NSAIDs historically, but the patient ran out of medication two days ago, precipitating this severe flare. Patient reports marked systemic inflammation, presenting as profound lethargy and mild subjective fever.
Functional Status: Hands are functionally useless tonight; unable to perform fine motor tasks or grip firmly.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; inability to open medication bottles or safely prepare meals.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Fatigued, guarding both hands closely to the chest, wincing when attempting to move fingers.
Physical Exam: Bilateral wrists, metacarpophalangeal (MCP), and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints demonstrate marked symmetrical swelling, warmth, and sponginess (synovitis). Tender to palpation and painful on all active/passive ranges of motion. No erythema or red streaking extending up the forearm. Normal capillary refill. Presentation strongly supports inflammatory arthritis lacking RF/CCP (seronegative RA).
Environmental Observations: Pre-packaged food left unopened on the counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Usually takes Meloxicam 15mg but exhausted supply 48 hours ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild Rheumatoid Arthritis Seronegative - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Infectious Tenosynovitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical presentation rather than isolated to a single tendon sheath, and lack of severe, tracking erythema.

Osteoarthritis Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominent soft, spongy swelling (synovitis) rather than hard bony nodes, prominent MCP involvement, and the presence of prolonged morning stiffness and systemic fatigue.

Gout - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, polyarticular small joint presentation in the upper limbs, rather than classical monoarticular lower limb presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's sudden, severe loss of upper limb function due to symmetrical joint swelling required immediate after-hours assessment. It was clinically vital to rule out rapidly destructive bacterial infections like septic arthritis or flexor tenosynovitis. Once a severe inflammatory autoimmune flare was confirmed—precipitated by medication cessation—urgent administration of potent systemic anti-inflammatories was required to halt the synovial inflammation, restore functional grip, and prevent severe distress and dehydration overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg for rapid reduction of acute synovial inflammation. Issued an urgent prescription to resume Meloxicam 15mg daily. Targeted intervention strategy includes COX-2 inhibitors, Wahls Diet implementation, and Vitamin D.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your anti-inflammatory medication directly with a full meal every single morning, you will actively prevent stomach irritation while maintaining the necessary drug levels to suppress joint swelling, rewarding yourself with functional, pain-free hands throughout the day.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Replaced tightly capped water bottles with an open cup for tonight.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 3 days for rheumatology referral and optimization of disease-modifying agents (DMARDs).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department via 000 if a single joint becomes disproportionately swollen, turns bright red, or if high fever and uncontrollable shivering develop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A diagnosis of seronegative RA is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion and requires formal rheumatological evaluation and specialized blood/imaging workup.
Clinical Course: The acute joint swelling will decrease significantly over the next 12-24 hours with the resumption of high-dose anti-inflammatories.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if your hand becomes fiery red with red streaks moving up your arm, or if you develop a severe fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lack of medication allowed your immune system to aggressively attack the linings of your joints, filling them with inflammatory fluid; the injection acts to quickly drain this fluid and turn off the pain receptors.
Actionable Steps: Rest your hands completely tonight, avoiding any tight gripping; resting the inflamed tendons prevents secondary micro-tears, positively reinforcing a faster return to normal hand function tomorrow.

